    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\log_{4}(\\sqrt{4 x}),$ find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\log_{4}(u),\\quad u=\\sqrt{4 x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\ln(4)},\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4 x}}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\ &=\\frac{1}{u\\ln(4)}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4 x}})\\\\ &=\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{4 x})\\ln(4)}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4 x}})\\\\ &=\\frac{4}{2(4 x)\\ln(4)}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\cancel{4}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{4}x\\ln(4)}\\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x \\ln{(4 )}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
